#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f26d941b-b4b4-591e-b7e0-ae4bbf81e94b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38252138-248d-5fc1-8988-a3ff2efa765a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t{e}^{st}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337184} {:answer/id #uuid "2eaefb92-722a-5398-b368-816bb03c8e1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337185} {:answer/id #uuid "15f476e0-2b06-5912-9ee4-083657a9e6ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t({e}^{st}-{e}^{-st})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337186} {:answer/id #uuid "bf607762-cc10-5ab7-8db3-61ffc1f90228" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t{e}^{-st}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337187} {:answer/id #uuid "99ecf009-aac0-5449-b33a-6e78ebbf08df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t({e}^{2st}+{e}^{-2st})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15f476e0-2b06-5912-9ee4-083657a9e6ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t({e}^{st}-{e}^{-st})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337186} :answer-field/id #uuid "97f83934-c065-5d6e-b769-2db25e1663f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1fdfd0b6-2c3b-50ac-bc5c-0d29eeafd8d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-94747" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{y},$ where $x={e}^{st}$ and $y={e}^{-st}.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059013/assets/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{1}{({e}^{st})^{2}}=-{e}^{-2st}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\frac{1}{({e}^{-st})^{2}}=-{e}^{2st}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =t{e}^{st}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =-t{e}^{-st}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-2st})(t{e}^{st})+(-{e}^{2st})(-t{e}^{-st}) \\\\  & =t{e}^{st}-t{e}^{-st} \\\\  & =t({e}^{st}-{e}^{-st}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c243ca-5d46-58d2-bd16-28075c04d2db" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function of Three Intermediate Variables and Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "916f71d9-1461-5cbc-a96a-fa2facdfc84f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t + 4s - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165152} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf9c459-9ead-5506-8296-67e11bcaa57b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t - 3s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165153} {:answer/id #uuid "cff4bcaf-76ab-55eb-8c51-2853168f6c59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165154} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6ff427-9bf3-58ed-9f8f-8edd6d37b239" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 - 8s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165155} {:answer/id #uuid "5998c176-c992-5217-8977-1683ab72fccb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5998c176-c992-5217-8977-1683ab72fccb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165156} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7ab6d1c-5493-5461-9355-8385cc0e3f0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db13dec0-b66e-50d3-9ee6-bdc2288dd174" :question/math-academy-id "q-94814" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=4x+3y-2z,$ where $x=2{s}^{2}-t,$ $y=s+3{t}^{2}$ and $z=4{s}^{2}+{t}^{2}.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059013/assets/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =4s, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =8s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(4)\\cdot 4s+(3)\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 8s \\\\  & =16s+3-16s \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
